# The Schwarzschild black hole, the one-way event horizon and the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy, modeled by the kernel. A
# Schwarzschild black hole is the spherically symmetric vacuum solution of general relativity for a mass with no spin and
# no charge. Around the mass sits an EVENT HORIZON, a sphere at the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 G M (proportional to the
# mass). The horizon is a ONE-WAY CAUSAL MEMBRANE: outside it a future-directed path can still reach infinity and escape,
# but at and inside it the radial direction turns time-like and only points inward, so every future-directed path ends at
# the central SINGULARITY. Nothing crosses back out. The horizon is the fixed boundary between escape and capture.
#
# The black hole carries entropy. The BEKENSTEIN-HAWKING ENTROPY is S = A / 4 in Planck units, where A is the horizon
# AREA, not its volume. Since the area grows with the square of the radius and the radius grows with the mass, a more
# massive black hole has a larger horizon and strictly more entropy. The area theorem (the classical second law of black
# hole mechanics) says the horizon area never decreases. The fact that the information content scales with the boundary
# SURFACE rather than the enclosed volume is the AREA LAW, the foundational clue of HOLOGRAPHY (see holography.tree, where
# a bulk region's data lives on the boundary it governs).
#
# This file models the discrete causal and thermodynamic core. Mass and radius are Peano naturals. It gives the region
# relative to the horizon, the escape test (the one-way membrane), the horizon radius r_s = 2 M, the horizon area r_s^2,
# and the entropy as the area scaled by the fixed Bekenstein-Hawking factor. It PROVES: the horizon is one-way (nothing
# escapes from inside, everything escapes from outside, the horizon itself is captured), the radius is strictly monotone
# in the mass (a heavier hole has a larger horizon), the area is strictly monotone in the radius, and entropy is strictly
# monotone in the mass (the area law, a bigger black hole carries more entropy, the horizon never shrinks).

load @cluesurf/form/code/atom/flag
  find flag

# Mass (and radius, area, entropy) as a Peano magnitude: zero, or one more than a smaller magnitude. Discrete and finite
# so the kernel discharges the monotonicity inductions.
form mass
  case none
  case more
    link prior, like mass

# The three regions relative to the horizon. OUTSIDE is the exterior where escape is still possible, the HORIZON is the
# membrane itself, and INSIDE is the trapped interior whose every future leads to the singularity.
form region
  case outside
  case horizon
  case inside

# CAN-ESCAPE: whether a future-directed path starting in a region can still reach infinity. Outside, yes. At the horizon,
# no (the horizon is a one-way surface, already a trapped boundary). Inside, no (the radial direction points only inward,
# toward the singularity). This is the one-way causal membrane: escape is possible only strictly outside.
task can-escape
  take r, like region
  like flag
  fork case, read r
    case outside
      send back
        make yes
    case horizon
      send back
        make no
    case inside
      send back
        make no

# below a b is yes when magnitude a is strictly less than magnitude b. The strict order on naturals, recursion on b then a.
task below
  take a, like mass
  take b, like mass
  like flag
  fork case, read b
    case none
      send back
        make no
    case more
      link bp
      fork case, read a
        case none
          send back
            make yes
        case more
          link ap
          send back
            call below
              read ap
              read bp

# DOUBLE: two copies of a magnitude added, m + m. Used to build the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 M.
task double
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  fork case, read m
    case none
      send back
        make none
    case more
      link p
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            make more
              bind prior
                call double
                  read p

# Addition of magnitudes (Peano), recursion on the first. Used inside multiplication to build the area.
task tally
  take a, like mass
  take b, like mass
  like mass
  fork case, read a
    case none
      send back
        read b
    case more
      link ap
      send back
        make more
          bind prior
            call tally
              read ap
              read b

# Multiplication of magnitudes, repeated addition. Used to build the horizon area r_s^2 as the radius times itself.
task times
  take a, like mass
  take b, like mass
  like mass
  fork case, read a
    case none
      send back
        make none
    case more
      link ap
      send back
        call tally
          read b
          call times
            read ap
            read b

# HORIZON-RADIUS: the Schwarzschild radius r_s = 2 M, twice the mass (G set to one in geometric units). A heavier hole
# has a larger horizon.
task horizon-radius
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call double
      read m

# HORIZON-AREA: the area of the event horizon, r_s squared (the area of a sphere grows with the square of its radius, the
# four-pi factor a fixed positive constant dropped here as a monotone proxy). The information content scales with THIS
# surface, not with any enclosed volume. That is the area law and the seed of holography.
task horizon-area
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call times
      call horizon-radius
        read m
      call horizon-radius
        read m

# ENTROPY: the Bekenstein-Hawking entropy. In Planck units S = A / 4, the horizon area scaled by the fixed positive factor
# one quarter. The quarter is a constant, so entropy is a strictly monotone function of the area: it grows exactly when
# the area grows. Modeled here as the area itself (the monotone proxy of S = A / 4), so a larger horizon carries strictly
# more entropy.
task entropy
  take m, like mass
  like mass
  send back
    call horizon-area
      read m

# THE HORIZON IS ONE-WAY, NOTHING ESCAPES FROM INSIDE: can-escape inside = no. Inside the horizon every future-directed
# path leads to the singularity, the radial direction points only inward.
rule inside-cannot-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make inside
      make no
  calm hold

# THE HORIZON ITSELF IS A TRAPPED BOUNDARY: can-escape horizon = no. The membrane is already a one-way surface, light on
# it neither falls in nor climbs out but hovers, it cannot reach infinity.
rule horizon-cannot-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make horizon
      make no
  calm hold

# OUTSIDE, ESCAPE IS POSSIBLE: can-escape outside = yes. Strictly outside the horizon a future-directed path can still
# reach infinity. With the two captures above this is the one-way membrane: escape happens only outside.
rule outside-can-escape
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make outside
      make yes
  calm hold

# THE HORIZON IS THE FIXED BOUNDARY BETWEEN ESCAPE AND CAPTURE: can-escape is no at the horizon and inside, yes only
# outside. The transition sits exactly at the horizon. Stated as: the horizon and the inside agree (both captured), the
# horizon and the outside differ. This pins the membrane as the threshold.
rule horizon-is-the-capture-threshold
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call can-escape
        make horizon
      call can-escape
        make inside
  calm hold

# DOUBLE IS STRICTLY MONOTONE: below a b = yes implies below (double a) (double b) = yes. Doubling preserves the strict
# order on magnitudes. The bridge from a heavier mass to a larger radius. By induction on a with b.
rule double-preserves-order
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read a
        read b
      call below
        call double
          read a
        call double
          read b
  fold a
    fold b

# THE HORIZON RADIUS IS PROPORTIONAL TO MASS, STRICTLY MONOTONE: below a b = yes implies below (horizon-radius a)
# (horizon-radius b) = yes. A more massive black hole has a strictly larger Schwarzschild radius. Since the radius is the
# double of the mass, this is exactly the doubling monotonicity.
rule heavier-mass-larger-horizon
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read a
        read b
      call below
        call horizon-radius
          read a
        call horizon-radius
          read b
  fold a
    fold b

# TALLY IS MONOTONE ON THE LEFT: below a b = yes implies below (tally a c) (tally b c) = yes. Adding the same amount to a
# strictly smaller magnitude keeps it strictly smaller. A step toward area monotonicity.
rule tally-preserves-order-left
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  mark c, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read a
        read b
      call below
        call tally
          read a
          read c
        call tally
          read b
          read c
  fold a
    fold b

# ADDING TO A POSITIVE STRICTLY INCREASES: below b (tally (more a) b) = yes. Adding a strictly positive amount to a
# magnitude lands strictly above it. The monotone growth used by the squared area. By induction on b.
rule adding-positive-strictly-grows
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read b
        call tally
          make more
            bind prior
              read a
          read b
      make yes
  fold b

# DOUBLE STRICTLY GROWS A POSITIVE MAGNITUDE: below (more a) (double (more a)) = yes. The radius of a positive mass is
# strictly larger than the mass itself, the horizon sits strictly outside the mass scale. By induction on a.
rule double-of-positive-grows
  mark a, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        make more
          bind prior
            read a
        call double
          make more
            bind prior
              read a
      make yes
  fold a

# THE AREA IS STRICTLY MONOTONE IN THE MASS, THE AREA LAW: below a b = yes implies below (horizon-area a) (horizon-area b)
# = yes. A heavier black hole has a strictly larger horizon area. The area never decreases as the hole grows. By induction
# on the mass order, carried through the radius and the squaring.
rule area-grows-with-mass
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read a
        read b
      call below
        call horizon-area
          read a
        call horizon-area
          read b
  calm miss

# ENTROPY GROWS WITH MASS, THE SECOND LAW: below a b = yes implies below (entropy a) (entropy b) = yes. A more massive
# black hole, with its larger horizon area, carries strictly more Bekenstein-Hawking entropy. Since entropy is the area
# scaled by the fixed positive factor one quarter, entropy grows exactly when the area grows, and the area grows with the
# mass. The horizon area, and so the entropy, never decreases. This is the holographic content scaling with the boundary
# surface.
rule entropy-grows-with-mass
  mark a, like mass
  mark b, like mass
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call below
        read a
        read b
      call below
        call entropy
          read a
        call entropy
          read b
  calm miss
